    <item>
      <title>Rake build utility</title>
      <link>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/rake-build-utility/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 08:50:47 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/rake-build-utility/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://ruby.github.io/rake/&#34;  class=&#34;external-link&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;Rake&lt;/a&gt; is one of the best tools created in Ruby and widely used for running scripts. In my opinion, its beautiful DSL inspired the creation of other projects, like &lt;a href=&#34;https://capistranorb.com/&#34;  class=&#34;external-link&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;Capistrano&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;While Rake is mostly used for running build scripts, code checks, and tests, I believe it can also be used to some extent for running business tasks related to your project.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But there is one thing to keep in mind when writing your scripts: avoid exceeding 10-15 lines of code inside a task block. If your logic doesn&amp;rsquo;t fit within this limit, you can create a separate class to be used inside the task. Just make sure to place this class inside a namespace, because once loaded by the interpreter, it becomes globally available.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Checking background tasks</title>
      <link>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/checking-background-tasks/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 02 Jun 2026 06:58:50 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/checking-background-tasks/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;One common problem when working with background tasks in Rails is ensuring that the task queue itself is configured and functioning correctly.&#xA;For this purpose, I always create a test job that simply writes a test message to the log:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-ruby&#34; data-lang=&#34;ruby&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nc&#34;&gt;CheckJob&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;no&#34;&gt;ApplicationJob&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;  &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;queue_as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;ss&#34;&gt;:default&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;  &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;nf&#34;&gt;perform&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;no&#34;&gt;Logger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;no&#34;&gt;Rails&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;log&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;check.log&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;ss&#34;&gt;level&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;ss&#34;&gt;:info&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;    &lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;info&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;OK&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;  &lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;k&#34;&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Next, I run it in the console, for example like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-bash&#34; data-lang=&#34;bash&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;bin/rails r &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;CheckJob.perform_later&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;This check can also be extended by adding execution of this task to the cron using the &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/javan/whenever&#34;  class=&#34;external-link&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;whenever&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; gem:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ruby One-Liners</title>
      <link>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/ruby-one-liners/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 13 May 2026 07:58:47 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/ruby-one-liners/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;One of the coolest things about Ruby is that you can write clear yet concise code.&#xA;This is especially useful when you want to automate repetitive tasks using scripts.&#xA;To understand how to use Ruby in your scripts, in most cases you can simply look at the standard help for the &lt;code&gt;ruby&lt;/code&gt; command, such as &lt;code&gt;ruby -h&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;man ruby&lt;/code&gt;. You can also pass arbitrary code using the &lt;code&gt;-e&lt;/code&gt; option, for example:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
